Allow private subnets → outbound internet (for updates, Docker pulls, etc.) WITHOUT exposing them publicly.

🧠 Concept First (Important)

Flow:

Private Subnet → NAT Gateway → Internet Gateway → Internet

👉 NAT = outbound only
👉 IGW = inbound + outbound (public)

Step 1: Add Elastic IP (Required for NAT)

Open main.tf and add:

resource "aws_eip" "nat_eip" {
  domain = "vpc"
}

✍️ Step 2: Create NAT Gateway (in public subnet)
resource "aws_nat_gateway" "nat" {
  allocation_id = aws_eip.nat_eip.id
  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.public_1.id

  tags = {
    Name = "ecommerce-nat"
  }
}

✍️ Step 4: Associate Private Subnets
resource "aws_route_table_association" "private_1_assoc" {
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.private_1.id
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.private_rt.id
}

resource "aws_route_table_association" "private_2_assoc" {
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.private_2.id
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.private_rt.id
}

▶️ Step 5: Apply Terraform
terraform plan
terraform apply

Type:

yes

⏳ Wait Time

NAT Gateway takes 2–3 minutes to create.

Don’t panic if it’s slow.

✅ Step 6: Verify

In AWS Console:

NAT Gateway → Available
Private route table → 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT
Subnets associated properly

